<h1>Decruft</h1>
<p class="tag">Safe cleanup, more free disk space. For macOS.</p>

<p>Decruft finds the disk space you can get back — old build and package caches,
leftovers from applications you already uninstalled, large files you forgot, and
duplicates — and explains every item in plain words before anything is removed.</p>

<h2>Support</h2>
<p>Email <a href="mailto:xcnuowei@163.com">xcnuowei@163.com</a>. Please include your
macOS version and what you were doing at the time; it helps to have the name of
the category you were looking at.</p>

<h2>Questions</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Is anything deleted permanently?</dt>
  <dd>No. Whatever you remove is moved into the app's own storage and stays
      recoverable for 14 days from the Recently Removed list.</dd>

  <dt>Why can't it remove some applications?</dt>
  <dd>Applications installed by a package installer are owned by the system, and
      an App Store app is not allowed to gain the privileges needed to delete
      them. Those are listed with a button that reveals them in Finder, where you
      can drag them to the Trash yourself. Their leftover data can still be
      removed normally.</dd>

  <dt>Does it touch my source code or projects?</dt>
  <dd>No. The developer categories target each tool's own cache directory by a
      fixed path. Your repositories are never candidates for removal.</dd>

  <dt>Does it send anything over the internet?</dt>
  <dd>No. The app ships without the network entitlement, so it cannot. See the
      <a href="privacy.html">privacy policy</a>.</dd>

  <dt>What does the purchase cover?</dt>
  <dd>Scanning is free. Cleaning is a one-time purchase, not a subscription, and
      it applies to every Mac signed in to your Apple Account. If you have
      already bought it, use Restore Purchase in Settings.</dd>
</dl>

